--TEST--
What a peer sent arrives `tainted`, so it reaches no sink without laundering while `Core\Socket`'s own two sends still forward it — `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-loop`, `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3 marks a received frame's payload `tainted`: it is untrusted input off a
// network, exactly like a request body, and `Core\Validate` is the only way to
// launder it. What that costs is asserted here on both sides of one value, so
// a reader can tell the qualifier is on the payload rather than on the member
// that answered it.
//
// Forwarding it is accepted -- a frame is not an instruction on this side of
// the wire, so both sends classify their payload neutrally and § 3's own loop
// can hand back what the peer sent. Reaching a path with it is refused,
// because a path *is* an instruction (`rule:security/sink-predicate`), and the expression is the
// same one in both places.
class Chat {
    public static function relay(Core\Socket $conn, Core\Socket\Message $msg): void {
        if ($msg->topic() != null || $msg->value() != null) {
            return;
        }
        var $binary = $msg->bytes();
        if ($binary != null) {
            $conn->sendBytes($binary);
            return;
        }
        var $payload = $msg->text();
        if ($payload != null) {
            $conn->send($payload);
            Core\IO::read($payload);
        }
    }
}

try {
    var $conn = Core\Socket::current();
    var $msg = $conn->receive();
    if ($msg != null) {
        Chat::relay($conn, $msg);
    }
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:26:27
   |
26 |             Core\IO::read($payload);
   |                           ^^^^^^^^ this is `tainted string`

error: aborting due to 1 error
